# Scaffold templates are embedded into the CLI byte for byte and become the
# files a generated project contains. Git's autocrlf converts text files on
# checkout, so without this a clone on Windows gets CRLF templates, the CLI
# embeds CRLF, and every generated project differs from the one CI verified.
#
# Found the day the templates moved out of Go string literals (v3.338.0): the
# repair constants stopped matching the templates they mirror, and only on
# Windows, because CI runs on Linux where no conversion happens. The same shape
# as v3.333, where the compose repairs had never once run on a Windows checkout.
internal/scaffold/templates/** text eol=lf

# The scaffolder reads these and writes their contents into projects too.
*.tmpl text eol=lf

# Repair fixtures are files a repair matches character for character, which
# includes the line endings. The same conversion that broke the templates would
# make every anchor miss on a Windows clone.
internal/scaffold/testdata/** text eol=lf
internal/generate/testdata/** text eol=lf

# docs/components/error-codes.ts is written by tools/errorcodes and compared
# against that tool's output byte for byte by TestDocsTableIsCurrent. Without
# this, a Windows clone gets CRLF on checkout and the test fails on a file
# nobody has touched, telling you to regenerate something that is already
# correct. Found reviewing grit#184, where it looked like the contributor's
# mistake and was not: `git checkout -- docs/components/error-codes.ts` is
# enough to break it, and CI never sees it because CI runs on Linux.
docs/components/error-codes.ts text eol=lf
